Instruction: Review the following merger agreement excerpt and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to the carveouts set forth under the Material Adverse Effect provisions?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
"Company Material Adverse Effect" shall mean any event, circumstance, change, or effect which, individually or in aggregate with all other such events, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, operations, assets, liabilities, or financial condition of the Company, or which would in any manner prevent, materially delay, or impair the consummation of the transactions contemplated herein.
Provided, however, that for purposes of determining whether a Company Material Adverse Effect has occurred, none of the following shall be taken into account:
   (i) general changes in economic conditions or market fluctuations;
   (ii) changes in financial, credit, or capital markets conditions not peculiar to the Company;
   (iii) regulatory or legislative changes affecting entire industries;
   (iv) force majeure events such as natural disasters or epidemics;
   (v) any event that solely affects the trading volume or market price of the Company’s securities; and
   (vi) effects attributable to risks inherent to the industry as a whole.
Notwithstanding the foregoing exclusions, it is expressly provided that any event or effect that directly impairs or delays the Company’s ability to consummate the transaction shall not be excluded by these carveouts.

(End of Excerpt)
